WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.840
The insulin gene never leaves a beta cell's nucleus.

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.640
So how does insulin end up outside the cell?

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:17.020
After a meal, your blood sugar rises, and beta cells in your pancreas release this

4
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.020
protein hormone.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.120
Here is the answer in one picture.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.920
First a copy of the instructions travels, then the protein itself.

7
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.700
Each part can only work on what the part before it hands over.

8
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.073
Start in the nucleus.

9
00:00:34.373 --> 00:00:37.473
It holds most of the cell's DNA, its instructions.

10
00:00:38.893 --> 00:00:41.753
The gene for insulin is one stretch of that DNA.

11
00:00:43.093 --> 00:00:44.713
But the gene itself stays put.

12
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:47.487
Here is a puzzle for you.

13
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:50.427
The gene stays locked in the nucleus.

14
00:00:51.707 --> 00:00:55.387
So what leaves the nucleus instead, carrying its instructions out?

15
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.750
A copy. The cell copies the gene into messenger RNA.

16
00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.070
That working copy leaves the nucleus and carries the instructions to the next stop.

17
00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:16.863
The next stop is a ribosome on the rough ER.

18
00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:20.523
The ER is a set of folded membranes.

19
00:01:21.823 --> 00:01:24.503
On the rough ER, ribosomes dot the surface.

20
00:01:25.863 --> 00:01:31.123
A ribosome reads the messenger RNA and links building blocks into a protein chain.

21
00:01:32.343 --> 00:01:34.863
The chain threads into the ER, where it folds.

22
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:38.413
Now the protein has to move.

23
00:01:39.613 --> 00:01:43.013
A vesicle, a small bubble of membrane, buds off the ER.

24
00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:47.093
It carries the protein across to the Golgi apparatus.

25
00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:50.963
The Golgi is a stack of flat sacs.

26
00:01:51.663 --> 00:01:54.763
It sorts the protein and packs it into new vesicles.

27
00:01:56.103 --> 00:01:58.143
There the protein waits until it is needed.

28
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:02.413
At the last stop, a vesicle meets the cell membrane.

29
00:02:03.613 --> 00:02:04.853
The two membranes fuse.

30
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:09.633
The vesicle opens to the outside, and insulin is released.

31
00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.107
None of this is free.

32
00:02:13.487 --> 00:02:17.127
Building a protein chain and moving vesicles both use energy.

33
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:23.167
Much of that energy comes as ATP, a small molecule cell parts can use.

34
00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:28.127
Mitochondria release energy from sugar and store it in ATP.

35
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.790
Now look at the whole route.

36
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.450
The nucleus sends out a copy.

37
00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:39.610
Then the protein moves: rough ER, vesicle, Golgi, vesicle, cell membrane.

38
00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:42.973
Notice what a route like this means.

39
00:02:44.173 --> 00:02:47.553
Each part can only work on what the part before it hands over.

40
00:02:48.693 --> 00:02:51.233
So every step depends on the one before it.

41
00:02:52.183 --> 00:02:54.423
So how does insulin get out of the cell?

42
00:02:55.643 --> 00:02:57.263
No single part does it.

43
00:02:58.163 --> 00:03:03.163
A copy of the gene leaves the nucleus, and the protein is handed from part to part.

44
00:03:04.703 --> 00:03:06.803
Mitochondria supply much of the ATP.
